\chapter{Literature Review}

\section{Introduction}
This chapter provides a comprehensive and critical review of the existing literature related to IT Service Management (ITSM), specifically focusing on the evolution, implementation, and optimization of the ITIL 4 framework. As higher education institutions navigate complex digital environments, the integration of intelligent technologies becomes paramount. Therefore, this review systematically explores the intersection of Robotic Process Automation (RPA) and Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) in automating Key Performance Indicators (KPIs). Furthermore, it investigates the mathematical application of Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (MCDM) methods, with a specific focus on the Simultaneous Evaluation of Criteria and Alternatives (SECA) method, concluding with a synthesis that establishes the core research gap of this study.

\section{ITIL 4 and Modern ITSM Governance}
The transition from ITIL v3 to ITIL 4 represents a paradigm shift in computing governance, moving away from rigid, isolated lifecycle processes toward an integrated Service Value System (SVS). Empirical studies emphasize that while ITIL 4 provides an agile structural foundation for service delivery, organizations globally face systemic challenges in manual KPI tracking and data interpretation. 

In developing academic contexts, this challenge is further magnified. Institutions frequently suffer from a significant ``meaning gap'' where raw technical data generated by IT infrastructures is completely disconnected from strategic decision-making frameworks. Contemporary literature demonstrates that standard performance measurement practices, such as the Balanced Scorecard concept, often fail to capture the real-time operational realities of cloud-native systems without dynamic automation. Furthermore, managing large-scale cloud operations and conducting quality audits require model-based systems engineering methods to align service level management with capacity and performance management processes effectively.

\section{Robotic Process Automation (RPA) in Service Operations}
Robotic Process Automation (RPA) has emerged in recent computing literature as a highly effective non-invasive software technology designed to automate structured, repetitive, and rule-based tasks within the ITIL lifecycle. Empirical investigations into quality audit methodologies for IT support services suggest that manual metrics collection is a primary source of operational latency. 

By deploying software robots that emulate human actions, organizations can achieve a measurable reduction in human error and significantly minimize the Mean Time to Resolve (MTTR) critical incidents. Case studies within the literature, including automated frameworks developed for telecom and enterprise service desks, prove that RPA excels at automated incident logging, status synchronization, and standardized report generation. However, a consensus among researchers indicates a fundamental limitation: RPA lacks cognitive capabilities. When confronted with unstructured data—such as textual descriptions, user sentiments, or complex cloud incident patterns—RPA workflows require intelligent decision-making layers to avoid systemic execution failures.

\section{AI and GenAI for Intelligent KPI Assessment}
To overcome the deterministic limitations of standard automation, the integration of Artificial Intelligence, particularly Generative AI (GenAI) and Large Language Models, represents the contemporary frontier in ``Intelligent Governance.'' Recent computing literature (2023-2025) explores the utility of machine learning models in transforming traditional reactive incident management into proactive capacity management architectures. 

GenAI models are increasingly utilized to accurately detect, classify, and summarize complex semantic patterns hidden within technical support logs. By interpreting natural language inputs from users, GenAI allows for dynamic incident categorization, automated knowledge base cross-referencing, and intelligent root-cause insights. When synthesized with existing governance standards like COBIT 2019, AI-driven insights ensure that performance metrics are not merely recorded, but are contextually analyzed to predict system availabilities, mitigate future outages, and provide actionable intelligence for institutional leadership.

\section{The SECA Method and Multi-Criteria Evaluation}
A persistent challenge highlighted across ITSM literature is the objective ranking, selection, and prioritization of Key Performance Indicators (KPIs). In complex environments, IT managers are forced to evaluate multi-dimensional metrics that often present conflicting priorities (e.g., minimizing operational cost vs. maximizing system availability). 

Traditional Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (MCDM) methodologies, such as the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP), heavily depend on subjective expert weightings, which introduces cognitive bias and limits reproducibility. The Simultaneous Evaluation of Criteria and Alternatives (SECA) method, established in recent mathematical optimization modeling, addresses this critical flaw. SECA mathematically determines criteria weights and alternative scores simultaneously by maximizing the overall performance variance while minimizing internal correlations among metrics. The application of SECA within computing governance ensures that the selection of key ITIL metrics is entirely data-driven, objective, and legally justifiable within resource-constrained environments.

\section{Research Gap}
A thorough synthesis of the compiled literature reveals a clear, multi-faceted research gap that this study directly addresses:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{The Integration Gap:} While individual studies extensively document the isolated benefits of RPA for data processing, GenAI for cognitive log classification, and the SECA method for mathematical optimization, there is an absolute lack of a unified, end-to-end framework that integrates these three components into a single, automated ITIL 4 KPI assessment pipeline.
    \item \textbf{The Contextual and Localization Gap:} The vast majority of intelligent ITSM models are designed for well-funded, corporate cloud infrastructures. There is little to no empirical evidence regarding the application of integrated intelligent governance models within public higher education sectors in developing countries, specifically targeting the unique infrastructural and resource challenges faced by the Faculty of Computing and IT at Sana'a University.
\end{itemize}
